{"kind":"promise","text":"Claiming 7f3456ae. Verified the gap on current main 7e7b8412 before promising.\n\nIt is real and it is total. host.Workspace.Append at host/host.go:372 takes signer ed25519.PrivateKey and is the only append path on the public surface; the exported host API is Init, Open, Append, Records and the Application, Options, Act, Record, Log, Workspace types, and nothing among them accepts a record an outside process already signed. So today the only way for a browser signer to write is to hand the host the actor's private key, which is exactly the thing a browser signer exists to avoid.\n\nOne thing already in place that I will build on rather than reinvent: host/external_test.go compiles a real second module against this checkout and runs an application in it. Its own comment makes the point I would otherwise have had to argue -- an in-tree test proves nothing about the exported surface, because every package here may reach into internal/. The outside-module compile tests this request asks for extend that harness, and that is the only honest proof the boundary is sufficient.\n\nWhat I will expose: the minimum application-neutral data and operations an outside module needs to construct the exact canonical actor intent bytes -- binding payload tree, target genesis, schema, rests_on, idempotency namespace and key, attachments -- attach an Ed25519 actor signature outside the host process, and submit the already-signed request through the workspace sequencer. The host never sees the actor private key. The kernel/application boundary holds: no internal packages exported, no chess semantics, the server never rebuilds or re-signs actor intent, and sequencer signing and CAS stay inside the host.\n\nFail closed on every case named: wrong genesis, payload/tree mismatch, invalid or noncanonical signature or key, reserved binding schema, malformed identifiers, oversize payload or attachments, unsupported versions, idempotency conflicts, queue bounds. Bounded replay, commit and head information returned, enough for acknowledge-after-push.\n\nTests: outside-module compile tests, exact byte vectors, mutation-sensitive tamper tests, replay/conflict/concurrency tests, and public-doc examples. The byte vectors are the load-bearing ones -- a canonical-bytes contract that is only checked by round-tripping through our own encoder proves the encoder agrees with itself, so the vectors get pinned literally and the tamper tests must fail when a single byte of intent changes.\n\nThis changes the public host contract, so docs/reference/architecture.md is updated in the same head with its candidate artifact, plus exact-head artifacts for host. Gates: full and race Go tests, vet, gofmt, docs, git diff --check. Then independent review with Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions.\n\nOut of scope and I will not drift into it: JavaScript encoding, HTTP intake, writer lease, deploy recipe and browser storage all stay in item 6.\n\nOne scheduling note rather than an objection. The request says to sequence after chess item 5 lands. This work has no code dependency on chess -- it is entirely inside the gitseq host -- and item 4 is only just promised, so item 5 is some way off. I will respect the stated order, but if you want it earlier it can start now and would de-risk item 6 rather than block on it. Say so and I will bring it forward.\n\nBranch request/public-signed-submit in its own worktree, as the request records.","body":{"area":"host","base":"7e7b8412bcd95e3d3345a481b72febc502513109","branch":"request/public-signed-submit","request":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:7f3456ae8a539f03d1dcd28b85bbdc9e656a3e4c","sequencing":"request says after chess item 5; no code dependency on chess, can start earlier if wanted"}}